Behcet's disease
Rheumatology & Immunology
Recurrent oral ulcers, Genital ulcers, Uveitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-006
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of intense ocular pain, photophobia, and blurred vision, coupled with severe oral ulcerations preventing adequate oral intake.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a rapid evolution of a red, painful eye with vision changes (highly suspicious for acute uveitis) alongside debilitating mucosal ulcerations. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to initiate sight-saving management pathways and prevent severe dehydration, as the assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period without risking irreversible visual impairment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My mouth is full of sores so I can't even drink water, and now my right eye is throbbing and everything looks foggy."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 72-hour progressive history of multiple, exquisitely painful recurrent oral aphthous ulcers. Over the past 48 hours, the patient noticed the eruption of painful, erythema nodosum-like cutaneous lesions on the anterior lower limbs and a painful genital ulcer. Within the last 12 hours, the patient's right eye became intensely painful, red, and photophobic, with noticeably blurred vision. Denies any recent trauma to the eye. Reports a history of similar, though less severe, oral ulcers occurring at least three times a year.
Functional Status: Housebound, severely limited by ocular pain and inability to maintain oral nutrition.
Social/Safety Concerns: At risk of acute dehydration and rapid functional decline due to visual disturbance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears distressed, holding a hand over the right eye to block ambient light. Speaking with difficulty due to oral pain.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Multiple deep, erythematous-based aphthous ulcers present on the buccal mucosa and inner lips. Right eye exhibits marked conjunctival injection with a prominent ciliary flush and sluggish pupillary response, clinically indicating anterior uveitis. Genitourinary: A single, 1cm tender ulcer present on the genitalia. Dermatological: Tender, raised, erythematous nodules palpated on bilateral shins consistent with erythema nodosum. Integration of the diagnostic tetrad reveals the concurrent presence of recurrent oral ulcers, genital ulcers, and acute uveitis, highly suggestive of the underlying systemic condition pending formal pathergy testing.
Environmental Observations: Blinds drawn due to photophobia; uneaten meals present on bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted over-the-counter topical oral gels with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Behcet's disease - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Herpes Simplex Virus infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of concurrent non-vesicular erythema nodosum, classic anterior uveitis rather than dendritic keratitis, and the lack of a characteristic prodromal tingling.

Crohn's disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, or other focal gastrointestinal symptoms.

Syphilis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The excruciatingly painful nature of the genital and oral ulcers, sharply contrasting with the characteristically painless chancre of primary syphilis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, sight-threatening ocular complication (anterior uveitis) combined with severe mucocutaneous ulcerations that are compromising oral hydration. The rapid onset of visual disturbance and severe pain necessitated an immediate, after-hours clinical evaluation to expedite an urgent ophthalmology referral and initiate systemic corticosteroid therapy. Delaying this assessment until the next standard consultation window carried an unacceptable risk of permanent synechiae formation and irreversible visual loss, thus firmly satisfying the criteria for urgent intervention under MBS 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate referral to an ophthalmology emergency service or on-call ophthalmologist for slit-lamp evaluation and prescription of topical mydriatics and high-potency topical/systemic corticosteroids. Prescribe viscous lidocaine oral solution to enable fluid intake.
Positive Behavioral Change: Hydration scheduling: Apply topical oral anesthetic exactly 15 minutes before attempting to drink; achieving immediate, pain-free swallowing will positively reinforce the structured use of the medication and ensure fluid targets are met. Visual rest: Wear dark sunglasses indoors to immediately reduce photophobic pain, linking the physical barrier directly to symptom relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent specialist review within 12 hours. Routine GP review in 48 hours to monitor systemic response.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if there is a sudden, profound loss of vision, severe unremitting headache, neck stiffness, or confusion indicating potential central nervous system involvement.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical findings strongly suggest a systemic inflammatory disorder causing your eye and mouth symptoms, but specialized ophthalmological equipment is required to definitively stage the eye involvement.
Clinical Course: With prompt administration of specialized eye drops and systemic anti-inflammatories, the acute inflammation usually subsides, though the condition requires long-term specialist management to prevent relapses.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must be seen by an eye specialist immediately as arranged. If your vision suddenly goes black, or if you develop a severe headache and cannot touch your chin to your chest, go straight to the nearest emergency department.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The immediate priority is saving your vision and stopping the intense inflammation inside your eye, which requires specialized steroid drops. The oral numbing gel is strictly to allow you to drink water and prevent dehydration.
Actionable Steps: Use the numbing mouth gel 15 minutes before drinking. Keep the eye completely shielded from light. Proceed to the eye hospital immediately.

